<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tōdai-ji</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tōdai-ji is a Buddhist temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, built on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shomu">Emperor Shōmu</a> as the head of the state temples of the provinces. Its Great Buddha Hall houses a colossal bronze statue of the Buddha Vairocana, dedicated in 752. Rebuilt after fires, the hall is among the largest wooden buildings in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/todai-ji.jpg" alt="Tōdai-ji" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wiiii · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C5%8Ddai-ji_Kon-d%C5%8D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
